        "content_text": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5mac.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/6/2024/10/mac-mini-m4-desk1.jpeg?quality=82&#038;strip=all&#038;w=1600\" /></div><p>macOS Tahoe 26.5 was <a href=\"https://9to5mac.com/2026/05/11/macos-26-5-now-available-heres-everything-new/\">released today</a>, and while it’s light on changes, the update does include interesting new ‘Power control’ settings for compatible Mac desktops. And one might just be Apple’s answer to M4 Mac mini power button complaints.</p>\n\n\n\n <a data-layer-pagetype=\"post\" data-layer-postcategory=\"m4-mac-mini,macos-26-5,macos-tahoe\" data-layer-viewtype=\"unknown\" data-post-id=\"1051697\" href=\"https://9to5mac.com/2026/05/11/macos-26-5-adds-new-way-to-control-your-macs-power-state-via-switches/#more-1051697\" class=\"more-link\">more…</a>",
